WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.740
okay let's try a problem
regarding enthalpy of combustion this

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.870
one asks the standard enthalpies of
formation for carbon dioxide liquid

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:21.539
water and solid glucose at 25 degrees
Celsius are negative 400 negative 300

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:26.369
and negative 1,300 kilojoules per mole
respectively what is the standard

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:33.510
enthalpy of combustion per gram of
glucose at 25 degrees Celsius so if you

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.410
need to check out some of my thermo
chemistry tutorials to help you figure

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.700
this out and when you're ready give it a try

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.660
so first things first we're talking

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:03.000
about the standard enthalpy of
combustion and so this must be a

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.729
combustion reaction so let's write out
the combustion reaction we're performing

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:13.140
combustion on glucose so let's write
this out we have glucose which we know

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.360
is c6h12o6 and that's going to be a
solid and then combustion means we're

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:23.939
reacting with oxygen and so we're going
to need o2 gas and then in any

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:28.290
combustion we're always producing carbon
dioxide and water that is always going

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:33.000
to be the case and we need this to be
balanced so we have six six and six for

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.100
oxygen carbon dioxide and water so this
is our combustion reaction and now we

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:45.149
can do thermo chemical analysis on it as
we recall the standard enthalpy change

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:50.250
for any reaction is going to be equal to
the sum of the standard enthalpies of

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.930
formation of the products minus the sum
of the standard enthalpies of formation

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:59.310
of the reactants so let's go ahead and
plug in what we know

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:05.040
so first starting with the products
we're looking at carbon dioxide and we

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.670
know that that is negative 400 and we're
going to have to multiply that by six

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:13.240
because we have a coefficient of six in
the balanced equation

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.200
then we're adding to that because this
is a sum we're adding to that negative

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.770
300 for water again times six because we
have a coefficient of six then from all

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:28.300
of that we're going to subtract the
enthalpies of formation of the reactants

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:34.480
and so we have negative 1300 for glucose
and then we're going to have zero for

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.040
oxygen because as we know for any
element this is going to be zero we

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.830
don't have heats of formation for
elements only for compounds so we're

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:49.690
going to do the arithmetic and we find
that the enthalpy of combustion for

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:56.650
glucose is negative 2900 kilojoules per
mole so this is per mole of glucose but

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.300
the question asked per gram of glucose
so we have one more calculation to do

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.340
it's very simple we just have to take
negative 2900 kilojoules per mole and

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:12.070
then divide that by the molar mass of
glucose which is 180 grams per mole that

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:18.010
way the moles cancel and we're going to
end up with kilojoules per gram and we

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:25.580
do the arithmetic and we get negative
sixteen point one one kilojoules per gram of glucose.

